{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"Verified in SCOPe 2.08 (stable release; the current-release endpoint reports 2.08-stable with a periodic update labelled 2.08-2026-08-16): the three catalytic domains are placed in different FAMILIES of one SUPERFAMILY. The superfamily is c.55.1 "Actin-like ATPase domain" (sunid 53067). Its families are c.55.1.1 Actin/HSP70 (sunid 53068), c.55.1.3 Hexokinase (sunid 53083) and c.55.1.4 Glycerol kinase (sunid 53089). The same lineage covers all three: root, then class c "Alpha and beta proteins (a/b)" (sunid 51349), then fold c.55 "Ribonuclease H-like motif" (sunid 53066), then superfamily c.55.1. The superfamily also holds other families, for example c.55.1.2 Acetokinase-like, c.55.1.7 Glucokinase and c.55.1.10 ROK. I retrieved the superfamily lineage and its child families. I did not retrieve individual domain (SID) records for actin, HSP70, hexokinase or glycerol kinase.

Interpretation: a shared superfamily is SCOPe's level for a probable common evolutionary origin. It rests on structural and functional evidence even though sequence similarity is low. So the answer is that actin/HSP70, hexokinase and glycerol kinase are remote homologs. They are divergent members of the actin-like ATPase (ribonuclease H-like) lineage, and their different cellular roles do not argue against shared ancestry. Because they sit in different families, SCOPe does not claim the close, readily detectable relationship that a shared family implies. Family-level sharing is the stronger statement, and it is not made for these three. Beyond ancestry, you can infer only a conserved core architecture, the same fold, as the basis for a cautious structural comparison and for expecting a conserved nucleotide/phosphate-binding ATPase-like core.

Limits: the placement does not let you transfer a specific function, substrate, reaction or mechanism. Actin and HSP70 are not kinases for sugars or glycerol, and hexokinase (sugar) and glycerol kinase (glycerol) have different substrates. It does not make one structure an interchangeable template for another. Local active-site geometry, loops and inserted subdomains can differ, so a model from another family would need independent validation. Do not infer specificity, regulation, or sequence identity from the shared superfamily. The fold-level label (c.55) alone would indicate only structural similarity, but the superfamily level is what supports probable homology. SCOPe does not give the alignment or literature argument for the grouping in the API responses I used. The /homology endpoint returned 404 for the superfamily node, so I have no domain-level homology warnings to report, and I make no claim from their absence.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/children?limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/parents"]}]}
